---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";

const rows = [
  ["Everything the tree asserts", "24", "243", "Frederick I of Denmark, d. 1533",
   "Accepts both grafts below. This archive does not."],
  ["Accepting the Sneyd graft only", "17", "172", "Thomas Dabridgecourt of Longdon Hall, b. 1531",
   "Accepts the Keele descent, rejects Hornby."],
  ["Accepting the Hornby graft only", "24", "216", "Frederick I of Denmark",
   "Accepts the Holderness descent, rejects Keele."],
  ["What this archive can actually evidence", "≈7", "—", "as far as records this archive has read will carry it",
   "Rejects both grafts AND its own unevidenced upper storey. See below."],
];
---
<Base
  title="How Far Back"
  eyebrow="The question everyone asks first"
  dek="The family tree reaches the kings of Denmark in the fifteenth century. This archive reaches a Gloucestershire yeoman in 1654. This page explains the difference, which is two grafts and about a hundred people."

>
  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">"How far back does it go?" has four different answers for this family, and which
    one you get depends entirely on how much you are prepared to take on trust.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>If you accept…</th><th class="m">Generations</th><th class="m">Ancestors</th><th>Reaches</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map(([a, g, n, r, note], i) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{a}</strong></td>
              <td class="m">{g}</td>
              <td class="m">{n}</td>
              <td>{r}</td>
              <td class="small">{note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>Two grafts carry almost all the depth.</strong> Of 243 ancestors in the tree,
      <strong>71</strong> hang on a single unevidenced link in the D'Arcy line and
      <strong>27</strong> on a single disproved link in the Sneyd line. They do not overlap. Remove
      both and <strong>145</strong> people remain — which is still a substantial family, reaching the
      middle of the seventeenth century, and every one of them stands on something better than
      wishful thinking.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Every one of those 98 is now listed by name.</strong> The register marks them
      <em>above a graft</em> so you can see exactly who leaves when the joins do.
      <a href={u("/register")}>The register →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Re-audited 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">What a week of registers did to these numbers</h2>

    <p class="lede">This page's figures were an audit of what the archive could <em>defend</em>. Since
    then the two deepest lines have actually been searched, and the result moved in both directions
    at once.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>The line</th><th>Was</th><th>Is now</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>The Saniger line</strong><div class="small">the archive's oldest claim</div></td>
            <td class="small">twelve generations, resting on other people's trees — <em>untested</em></td>
            <td class="small"><strong>contradicted in the middle.</strong> The three Dursley baptisms
              that fit the tree's dates have three different fathers, and no Saniger baptism exists
              in Gloucestershire in the 1800s decade. <a href={u("/berkeley")}>→</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>The Sneyd line</strong><div class="small">after Keele Hall was disproved</div></td>
            <td class="small">resting on the 1742 Madeley baptism that killed the graft</td>
            <td class="small"><strong>possibly resting on a child who died.</strong> A William son of
              William Sneyd is buried at Madeley in September 1744.
              <a href={u("/sneyd")}>→</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>The D'Arcy line</strong><div class="small">Robert, and the Hornby graft above him</div></td>
            <td class="small">nothing between his 1776 commission and his 1827 will</td>
            <td class="small"><strong>better.</strong> Joseph D'Arcy baptised Portsea, 19 March 1780,
              father Robert, mother Jean. His marriage and his eldest son are documented.
              <a href={u("/hornby")}>→</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>The Wakefield join</strong><div class="small">how the family reaches England at all</div></td>
            <td class="small">one line in a Queensland death register, 1909</td>
            <td class="small"><strong>much better.</strong> The whole household found in the 1841
              census at St Paul, Bristol, with a Sinegar widow living in it.
              <a href={u("/drivers-fields")}>→</a></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>The honest figure has not moved, and the reason it has not moved has changed
      completely.</strong> It is still about seven generations. But a week ago the storey above that
      was <em>unproven</em>; now parts of it have been looked for in the right registers and found to
      be <em>wrong</em>. An unproven claim might yet be rescued by a document. A contradicted one has
      to be rebuilt. <a href={u("/searched")}>What was read, and what each source actually holds →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Graft one · unevidenced</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The D'Arcys of Hornby Castle</h2>
    <p>Above <strong>Major-General Robert D'Arcy</strong> the tree inserts a
    <em>Thomas (Francis) D'Arcy</em>, said to be born at St James's Palace, carrying two birth years
    sixty-nine years apart, and descends him from the Earls of Holderness. Through him come 71
    ancestors, including — by way of the Schombergs and the house of Oldenburg — the kings of
    Denmark.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">The tree's own note says Robert was "<em>thought to be the
    illegitimate</em>" son of the <strong>4th</strong> Earl, while its structure descends him from the
    <strong>3rd</strong>. No baptism for Robert exists in any indexed English collection; Connolly's
    <em>Roll</em>, which documents his whole career, records no parentage; the
    <em>Gentleman's Magazine</em> did not notice his death. <strong>Unevidenced, and set out in
    full.</strong> <a href={u("/hornby")}>The Hornby claim →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Graft two · disproved, 9 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The Sneyds of Keele Hall</h2>

    <p>This one the archive had flagged as suspect — <em>"Sneyd is a Staffordshire gentry name, which
    is exactly what a drifting pedigree reaches for"</em> — and then left alone. Tested, it fails, and
    it fails cleanly.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1rem">The line as the tree carries it runs down from Keele Hall to Kempsey:</p>

    <div class="spine" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <div class="gen undoc">
        <div><span class="yr">1723</span><span class="who">Ralph Sneyd, Esq., of Keele</span>
        <span class="whr"> · 1723–1793</span></div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;max-width:74ch">A real man, and a considerable one:
        born at Keele 10 December 1723, died at Bath 10 December 1793, squire of Keele Hall and the
        subject of a Keele University thesis. <strong>He is not in this family.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="gen undoc">
        <div><span class="yr">1742</span><span class="who">William Sneyd</span>
        <span class="whr"> · baptised All Saints, Madeley</span></div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;max-width:74ch">
          <span class="chip dis">the graft fails here</span> The tree makes him Ralph's son.
          The register does not.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gen">
        <div><span class="yr">1781</span><span class="who">Samuel Sneyd</span>
        <span class="whr"> · grocer, of Hanley</span></div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;max-width:74ch">Baptised Madeley, 4 June 1781,
        father William Sneyd. Married Elizabeth Margaret Oliver at Audlem, Cheshire, 22 October 1809.
        Died at No. 3 Well Street, Hanley, and was buried there in 1855.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gen">
        <div><span class="yr">1810</span><span class="who">Samuel Charles Sneyd</span>
        <span class="whr"> · 1810–1885</span></div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;max-width:74ch">Born Hanley, Stoke-on-Trent;
        christened there 28 April 1811. Came to New South Wales in <strong>1832 with the 4th (King's
        Own) Regiment</strong>; married Catherine Margaret Mulcahy at Camden, 13 February 1837. As a
        <strong>sergeant</strong> he and Private Richard Smith shot and shot a bushranger dead near the Vale of
        Clwydd on 30 October 1839 and took the other two. Sergeant-major of the Goulburn Division,
        chief constable at Moreton Bay from 1849, and <strong>Governor of Brisbane Gaol</strong> from
        1859. Died at Enoggera, 4 July 1885; buried Toowong.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gen">
        <div><span class="yr">1837</span><span class="who">William Hartley Sneyd</span>
        <span class="whr"> · Kempsey, New South Wales</span></div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;max-width:74ch">The emigrant generation's first
        Australian-born child, and Ivy Miriam Sneyd's grandfather. <a href={u("/sneyd")}>The Sneyds →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem">Three reasons the graft fails</h3>
    <ol class="dek">
      <li><strong>The baptism names a different father.</strong> The register of
      <strong>All Saints, Madeley</strong> records the baptism of <em>William Sneyd</em> in
      <strong>1742</strong> — father <strong>William Sneyd</strong>. Not Ralph. FamilySearch's own
      tree carries him as <em>William Snead II, 1742–1827</em>, matching a burial at Madeley on
      13 May 1827.</li>
      <li><strong>The chronology does not work.</strong> Ralph Sneyd of Keele was born in
      <strong>December 1723</strong>. A son baptised in 1742 would have been fathered when Ralph was
      <strong>eighteen</strong> — and at Madeley, a parish with no connection to him.</li>
      <li><strong>Ralph's actual children are on record, and William is not among them.</strong>
      Baptisms at <strong>Keele</strong> and <strong>Stone</strong> between 1750 and 1760 give Ralph
      Sneyd's children as Frances, Ralph, Edward, Anne, Elizabeth and Mary — by a wife named
      <strong>Barbara</strong>, not the "Margaret Dunning" the tree supplies. His documented heir was
      <strong>Walter Sneyd (1752–1829)</strong> of Keele.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>And the real line was there all along.</strong> The Madeley registers carry it:
      William Sneyd the elder, of Madeley in the 1720s–40s; his son <strong>William, baptised 1742</strong>,
      who <strong>married Mary Blackbourne at Madeley on 27 March 1769</strong> and was buried there in
      1827; their son <strong>Samuel, baptised 4 June 1781</strong>, the grocer of Hanley; and his son
      <strong>Samuel Charles</strong>, who sailed for New South Wales.
      <a href={u("/sneyd")}>Set out in full →</a> Losing Keele costs eleven generations of borrowed
      gentry and gains five of documented Staffordshire tradespeople.
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <strong>What was really going on.</strong> There were Sneyds at Keele Hall and there were
      Sneyds — often written <em>Snead</em> — at Madeley, five miles off, who were tradespeople. Our
      family is the second kind: a Madeley labouring or artisan family that produced a
      <strong>grocer at Hanley</strong>, whose son took the King's shilling and sailed for New South
      Wales. Somebody, at some point, joined the two on the strength of a shared surname in a shared
      county. Removing the join costs the archive eleven generations and a descent from Warwickshire
      gentry — and costs it nothing that was ever true.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">What survives</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Twelve generations, and a Gloucestershire yeoman</h2>
    <p>Strip out both grafts and <strong>145 ancestors</strong> remain, reaching
    <strong>generation twelve</strong>. The oldest of them are not aristocrats and there is no castle
    anywhere in the list:</p>
    <ul class="dek">
      <li><strong>William Sanigar</strong>, born 1654, and <strong>William Saniger</strong>, born at
      <strong>Berkeley, Gloucestershire</strong>, 12 October 1679 — reached through Hannah Saniger,
      Miriam Wakefield's mother, on the Bristol side.</li>
      <li><strong>Edward Cotton</strong>, born at Ashton-under-Hill, Gloucestershire, 6 January 1687.</li>
      <li><strong>Lydia Lyon</strong>, born at Market Drayton, Shropshire, 3 March 1683.</li>
      <li><strong>William Ward</strong> (1700) and <strong>Martha Lewis</strong> (1702) — the family
      of Jean Ward, whom Major-General Robert D'Arcy married at Portsea in 1779, and whose line does
      not depend on the Hornby graft at all.</li>
      <li><strong>William Garden</strong> and <strong>Jean Veitch</strong>, of Scotland.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Twelve generations to the 1650s, out of Gloucestershire, Shropshire,
    Staffordshire, Somerset and Scotland. That is a long way back by any ordinary standard. It is only
    disappointing if you had been promised Denmark.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Tested 10 September 2026 — and this one is ours</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">A third graft, and the archive built it itself</h2>

    <p class="lede">Everything above is about other people's mistakes. This section is about ours, and
    it is the most serious thing on this site.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1rem">The figure this page has been calling <strong>"what this archive will
    defend"</strong> — 144 ancestors, twelve generations, back to a Gloucestershire yeoman of 1654 —
    <strong>was never tested against the archive's own standard.</strong> When it finally was, on
    10 September 2026, it did not survive.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>What those 144 ancestors actually rest on</th><th class="m">People</th><th class="m">Share</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>A record set</strong> — a register, a census, a civil registration, a printed roll</td>
              <td class="m"><strong>8</strong></td><td class="m">5.6%</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Only other people's family trees</strong> — Guthrie Web Site, Saniger Web Site, FamilySearch Family Tree, Our Huge Family Tree</td>
              <td class="m"><strong>56</strong></td><td class="m">38.9%</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>No source at all</strong></td>
              <td class="m"><strong>79</strong></td><td class="m">54.9%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mixed or unclear</td><td class="m">1</td><td class="m">0.7%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>And the shape of it is worse than the total.</strong> Of the
      <strong>60 ancestors above generation seven</strong> — that is, everyone older than about 1780 —
      <strong>exactly one</strong> rests on a record set. The rest are family trees or nothing.
      <br /><br />
      Above generation seven this archive has been repeating other people's genealogy and calling it
      its own honest figure.
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem">The <a href={u("/sources")}>sources page</a> has said from the start
    that <strong>"a family tree is not a source"</strong>, and that a claim resting only on one is
    marked <span class="chip lore">family lore</span> however many trees repeat it. That rule was
    applied to the Hornby descent and to Keele Hall. <strong>It was never applied to the branch this
    page holds up as solid ground.</strong></p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem">The deepest line, and what it is made of</h3>
    <p>The Sanigers of Berkeley are the reason this page claims 1654. Six generations, every one with
    an exact christening date, running Berkeley → Elmore → Ashton-under-Hill → Wotton-under-Edge →
    Brimpsfield → Bisley, with Market Drayton in Shropshire married in.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th class="m">Date given</th><th>Place</th><th>Rests on</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Hannah Saniger</td><td class="m">b. 23 Jul 1805</td><td>Berkeley</td><td>Langford Family Site · FamilySearch Family Tree</td></tr>
          <tr><td>John Saniger</td><td class="m">b. 15 May 1769</td><td>Berkeley</td><td>Guthrie Web Site · FamilySearch Family Tree</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Thomas Saniger</td><td class="m">b. 23 Aug 1744</td><td>christened Berkeley</td><td>Saniger Web Site · FamilySearch Family Tree</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Thomas Saniger</td><td class="m">b. 11 Dec 1711</td><td>christened Berkeley</td><td>Guthrie Web Site</td></tr>
          <tr><td>William Saniger</td><td class="m">b. 12 Oct 1679</td><td>Berkeley</td><td>Saniger Web Site · FamilySearch Family Tree</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>William Sanigar</strong></td><td class="m"><strong>b. 1654</strong></td><td>—</td><td><strong>Gainard Family Tree Web Site</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.9rem">Those dates are precise, consistent and entirely
    plausible. They may well all be right — Berkeley's registers survive and somebody has clearly read
    them. <strong>But this archive has not seen them</strong>, and a date copied from a stranger's
    website is not evidence just because it has a day and a month on it.
    <span class="chip lore">family lore</span></p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>This has now been attempted — see <a href={u("/berkeley")}>Berkeley,
      Gloucestershire</a>.</strong> FreeREG's Berkeley transcript runs 1600–1677, so it can test only
      the oldest of the six generations. It produces one good candidate — William Sainger, baptised
      24 May 1655, son of <strong>Edward Sainger of Wanswell</strong> — among three Williams, and
      leaves the other five generations exactly where they were.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>What would still fix it.</strong> The parish registers of <strong>Berkeley,
      Gloucestershire</strong>, held at Gloucestershire Archives and substantially transcribed on
      FreeREG. Six christenings between 1654 and 1805 would turn the deepest line in this archive from
      hearsay into the best-evidenced thing on the site. It is one afternoon's work by somebody who
      can reach the registers. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Queued at the top →</a>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>Until then, the honest figure is not twelve generations.</strong>
    It is about <strong>seven</strong> — as far as the records this archive has actually read will
    carry it — with everything above that marked as what it is.
    <a href={u("/what-we-got-wrong")}>Recorded with the rest of our errors →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>The same shape twice</h2>
    <p class="dek">Both grafts have an identical structure, and it is worth naming because it is the
    commonest failure in family history. A <strong>documented working family</strong> — a Royal
    Engineer, a Hanley grocer — is traced back correctly for several generations. Then, at the point
    where the parish records thin out, it is <strong>joined to a landed family of the same surname in
    the same county</strong>, on no evidence, and the landed family's own well-published pedigree
    carries the line back another ten or fifteen generations for free.</p>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.9rem">The join is always the weakest link and always the least
    examined, because everything above it looks so solid — and it <em>is</em> solid. The Earls of
    Holderness are real. Ralph Sneyd of Keele is real. Neither of them is ours.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><a href={u("/method")}>How this archive labels claims →</a> ·
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>What is still open →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
